  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes the ecological roles and fate of domoic acid produced by the diatom *Pseudo-nitzschia* in sunlit surface waters. The central idea is that irradiance promotes photo-oxidation, linking light exposure to transformations of domoic acid and a detoxification product labeled PAC. Around this chemical pathway, the diagram highlights two major biological functions. On the left, domoic acid is associated with grazer defense against copepods, suggesting protection from zooplankton predation. On the right, it is linked to allelopathy, indicating inhibitory effects on a competing diatom, *Skeletonema* sp.\n\nAt the bottom, the figure shows copepodamide, a copepod-derived chemical cue, feeding into an “alarm cue” pathway that appears to stimulate *Pseudo-nitzschia* and promote domoic acid-related responses. Small bacteria-like shapes around the diatom chain imply interactions with microbes, although the details are not fully clear. The overall structure presents domoic acid as a multifunctional compound whose production is influenced by predator signals and whose activity is modified by sunlight, connecting chemical ecology, competition, and detoxification in marine plankton communities.",
